#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257b433d-960b-5602-9139-f0a08f725b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269602} {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} {:answer/id #uuid "a28f67e7-b07f-5e99-99f1-6ef0ca27df71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270719} {:answer/id #uuid "ab496260-2eb5-5f24-b36a-b5c83e218fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270799} {:answer/id #uuid "cee52816-3d9a-5a2d-bf35-0841387ab28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2912ecc2-b82b-545b-a134-0f849b9f6f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269638} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a50b6d4-0ed8-59a9-bfe9-13d431937f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf86f867-a1a5-56dc-89e8-e2255e71399b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139689" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c52f315d079b82fb729c4ab47f210ec4bf2683d0dfe1adfe79c94db9fbc5f63.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $a$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $b$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $c$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+0=2.\n$$"}]}
